.TH SHIMLINKS 1 "August 2026" "shimlinks VERSION" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shimlinks \- run commands with dotfile/state dirs pointing somewhere else
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shimlinks
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B shim-name
.RI [ args ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shimlinks
runs a command while its dotfile/state directories temporarily point
somewhere else.
.PP
The approach is inspired by boxxy, but instead of redirecting file
operations with Linux namespaces it uses symlink shims: a shim is a symlink in
the shimdir that points at this binary.  When the binary is invoked through a
shim (by its shim name), it:
.IP "1." 4
looks up the rules that match the shim name
.IP "2." 4
makes each matching rule's
.B target
a symlink pointing at its
.B rewrite
.IP "3." 4
runs the requested command with those symlinks in place
.IP "4." 4
removes those symlinks when the command exits
.PP
The exit status of the wrapped command is preserved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-install
Create a shim for every
.B only
name in the config.
Safe to re-run; existing shims that already point at this program are left
alone, and foreign files in the shimdir are not touched.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Remove shims in the shimdir that are no longer listed in the config.
Foreign files in the shimdir are left alone.
.TP
.B \-h\fP, \fB\-\-help
Show a short help message.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file is written in YAML.  Its location is resolved in this
order:
.IP "1." 4
.BR $SHIMLINKS_CONFIG
.IP "2." 4
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/shimlinks/config.yaml
.IP "3." 4
.BR ~/.config/shimlinks/config.yaml .
.PP
The format follows boxxy, so an existing boxxy config can be reused with
minimal changes.  The keys we use are:
.TP
.B shimdir
Directory where shims live.
It must be on your
.BR PATH ,
or the program refuses to run (see EXIT STATUS).
It should appear first, so the shims are found before any real command.
.TP
.B rules
List of rules.
Each rule consists of a
.B target,
a
.B rewrite,
and an
.B only
list.
.TP
.B target
The dotfile/state path to redirect.
While the command runs, it is made a symlink pointing at its
.B rewrite.
.TP
.B rewrite
The location that
.B target
points at while the command runs.
.TP
.B only
The command names that use this rule.
Each name becomes a shim in the shimdir.
.PP
The
.B name
and
.B mode
keys from boxxy configs are accepted but do nothing here, so they can be
ignored.
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimdir: ~/.local/shimlinks
rules:
  \- target: ~/.java
    rewrite: ~/.local/share/java
    only:
      \- java
  \- target: ~/.gnupg
    rewrite: ~/.local/share/gnupg
    only:
      \- gpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
A rule without an
.B only
list creates no shims and is ignored.
A malformed config aborts with an error instead of running with a partial
config.
.PP
Only the block format shown above is supported; anything else is rejected
with an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHIMLINKS_CONFIG
Path to the config file, overriding the defaults (see CONFIGURATION).
.TP
.B SHIMLINKS_DEBUG
When set to 1, print verbose per-step logging to stderr.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage, config, or setup error (missing or invalid config, shimdir not on
.BR PATH ,
shimdir not a directory, refusing to run a program by name, or refusing to
exec the program itself).
.TP
.B 2
Unknown command-line option.
.TP
.B 127
The wrapped command was not found on
.BR PATH ,
or failed to exec.
.TP
.B other
The wrapped command's own exit status (128 + signal number if it was killed by
a signal).
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up:
.IP "1." 4
Write
.B ~/.config/shimlinks/config.yaml
(see CONFIGURATION).
.IP "2." 4
Make sure the shimdir is on your
.BR PATH ,
for example:
.RS
.nf
export PATH="$HOME/.local/shimlinks:$PATH"
.fi
.RE
.IP "3." 4
Create the shims:
.RS
.nf
shimlinks --install
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a command through a shim:
.RS
.nf
java \-jar app.jar
.fi
.RE
.PP
While
.B java
runs,
.B ~/.java
is a symlink to
.BR ~/.local/share/java .
The symlink is removed when
.B java
exits.
.PP
Remove stale shims:
.RS
.nf
shimlinks --uninstall
.fi
.RE
